{#-
  Wildcat V2 markets — LENDER side.

  A Wildcat market is an uncollateralised credit line, not an ERC-4626 vault:
  the market contract is itself the position token (`wmtWETH` for the
  Wintermute WETH market) and exits run through a batched withdrawal queue.
  No ERC-4626 template applies.

  Each entry in `markets` is a market alias KEY. The template resolves it via
  aliases.wildcat[<key>] with NO existence guard. If the alias is missing on
  the active chain, rendering throws — there is no silent fall-through. To
  grant the Safe access to a market, add the key to `markets` AND ensure the
  matching entry exists in aliases/<chain>/wildcat.yaml.

  Each resolved alias entry must declare:
    address — the WildcatMarket contract
    asset   — the TOKEN ALIAS KEY of the market's underlying (e.g. "WETH");
              resolved via aliases.tokens[<asset>], also with no guard.

  Required template params:
    markets — list of market alias keys, e.g. ["wintermute_weth"].
              - aliases.wildcat[<key>]              must exist
              - aliases.tokens[<resolved asset>]    must exist
              Either lookup missing ⇒ render-time error.
              Duplicate keys are collapsed.

  Lender flow (request-then-claim, three steps):
    Deposit:
      1. approve(market, amount)   — on the underlying
      2. depositUpTo(amount)       — deposits up to remaining capacity and
                                     returns the amount actually taken, or
         deposit(amount)           — deposits exactly `amount` or reverts
                                     with `MaxSupplyExceeded`
      Market tokens mint to `msg.sender`; neither entry point takes a
      recipient, so shares can only ever land on the Safe.
    Exit:
      1. queueWithdrawal(amount) / queueFullWithdrawal()
                                   — burn market tokens into the current
                                     withdrawal batch and return that batch's
                                     `expiry`. Available liquidity is applied
                                     to the batch at queue time.
      2. (wait until block.timestamp >= expiry)
      3. executeWithdrawal(avatar, expiry)
                                   — transfer the batch's paid share of the
                                     underlying to `accountAddress`.
    The wait is the market's own `withdrawalBatchDuration`, an immutable set
    at market creation — read it off the market rather than assuming a value.

  Both deposit entry points and both queue entry points forward any calldata
  past their declared arguments to the market's hooks contract as `hooksData`
  (the market passes a `baseCalldataSize` of 0x24 / 0x04 into its internal
  path). A market whose access-control hooks use a signature-based role
  provider needs that trailing blob to authorise the lender.

  The roles below admit those longer calls, and that is not a decision this
  template made: Roles V2 scopes by ABI position and its `calldataMatches`
  places no upper bound on calldata length, so the DSL has no way to bound
  the blob. Admitting it happens to be what this market needs, and
  `test_deposit_withTrailingHooksData_allowed` pins it so a future change to
  the modifier's behaviour would surface — but read that test as a record of
  what the modifier does, not as evidence the length could have been
  constrained here.

  Output shape:
    - One approve role per UNIQUE underlying. Multiple markets sharing an
      underlying are aggregated into a single
      approve(spender = oneOf(market_addresses)) role. This is required —
      the emit-phase merger rejects duplicate (address, signature) pairs
      within a role key.
    - One role per market. The only address parameter on the whole lender
      surface is `executeWithdrawal`'s `accountAddress`, pinned to the avatar
      so a claim can only ever settle the Safe's OWN batch. That is narrower
      than "can only pay the Safe" and it is the property the pin actually
      buys: where the settled funds land is the market's decision, not the
      policy's. Its `expiry` names a batch, not a counterparty, so the batch
      selector is left open.

  Composing with other templates:
    The approve role targets the UNDERLYING, so a sibling config in the same
    safe-dir that also approves that token — `lagoon.tmpl` emits
    approve(spender = vault) on the vault's `asset` — must use a DIFFERENT
    role key. The emit-phase merger aggregates per role key and throws on a
    duplicate (address, signature) pair within one key, so two templates
    approving the same token under one key fail `generate`.

  Deliberately NOT granted:
    - executeWithdrawals(address[], uint32[]) — the batch-claim variant. Its
      `accountAddresses` array cannot be pinned to the avatar: `apply` rejects
      every `array_*` operator (see `buildScoping` in
      action/apply/toSdkTargets.ts), and leaving the slot `pass` would let the
      Safe settle arbitrary third parties' withdrawals. Claim one batch at a
      time with `executeWithdrawal` instead.
    - updateState(), collectFees(), repay(uint256) — permissionless on the
      market, so any address can call them and the Safe gains nothing from
      holding the grant. `repay` would additionally send the Safe's underlying
      to the market as an unrecoverable gift.
    - borrow, closeMarket, rescueTokens, setMaxTotalSupply,
      setAnnualInterestAndReserveRatioBips — `onlyBorrower`. All five are
      present on the deployed market and each has a negative test.
    - nukeFromOrbit(address), repayAndProcessUnpaidWithdrawalBatches(uint256,
      uint256) — permissionless, like `updateState`. Named here because both
      sit on the claim path and a reader will wonder: after a batch expires
      UNPAID, funding it is anyone's call to make, so the keeper does not need
      the Safe to hold either grant. `executeWithdrawal` then claims the old
      batch — its `expiry` is `pass`, so no extra grant is needed for that
      either.
    - setProtocolFeeBips(uint16), changeSphereXEngine(address) — protocol-level,
      gated to the factory and the SphereX operator respectively.
    - approve / transfer / transferFrom on the MARKET token. The market token
      is the position; moving it off the Safe is precisely what this policy
      exists to prevent. Note the underlying `approve` role below targets the
      UNDERLYING, never the market token.

  On deriving this list:
    The granted and not-granted sets above are taken from the market's VERIFIED
    ABI, cross-checked behaviourally against the deployed contract (a function
    that is present reverts with its own error selector — `rescueTokens` from a
    non-borrower gives `NotApprovedBorrower()` `0x02171e6a` — while an absent
    selector reverts with no data at all).

    A PUSH4 scan of the runtime bytecode is NOT a sound way to do this and an
    earlier revision of this docstring was wrong because of one: solc emits a
    selector whose leading byte is 0x00 as PUSH3, so such a function is
    invisible to that scan. `rescueTokens(address)` is exactly that case —
    `0x00ae3bf8`, emitted here as `62ae3bf8` (`PUSH3 0xae3bf8`) in the first
    branch of the dispatch table. It was briefly dropped from this list as
    "not present on this market". It is present. Scan PUSH1..PUSH4 if you scan
    at all, and prefer the ABI.
-#}
{%- from "_macros/common.tmpl" import approve -%}
{#- Pass 0: collapse duplicate market keys. A repeated key would emit the
    market's role block twice, and the emit-phase merger rejects a duplicate
    (address, signature) pair within a role key — so the duplicate fails
    `generate` with a message naming a FUNCTION rather than the key the author
    actually repeated. It would also push the same address into `spenders`
    twice, producing a `oneOf` with a repeated value. Collapsing here makes a
    repeat a no-op instead. -#}
{%- set unique_markets = [] -%}
{%- for key in markets -%}
  {%- if key not in unique_markets -%}
    {%- set _ = unique_markets.push(key) -%}
  {%- endif -%}
{%- endfor -%}
{#- Pass 1: build the unique list of underlying token alias keys -#}
{%- set assets = [] -%}
{%- for key in unique_markets -%}
  {%- set market = aliases.wildcat[key] -%}
  {%- if market.asset not in assets -%}
    {%- set _ = assets.push(market.asset) -%}
  {%- endif -%}
{%- endfor -%}
roles:
{%- for asset in assets %}
  {#- Pass 2: gather every market address whose underlying == asset -#}
  {%- set spenders = [] -%}
  {%- for k in unique_markets -%}
    {%- set m = aliases.wildcat[k] -%}
    {%- if m.asset == asset -%}
      {%- set _ = spenders.push(m.address) -%}
    {%- endif -%}
  {%- endfor %}
  {{ approve(aliases.tokens[asset], spenders) | indent(2) }}
{%- endfor %}
{%- for key in unique_markets %}
{%- set market = aliases.wildcat[key] %}

  - address: "{{ market.address }}"
    functions:
      - signature: "function depositUpTo(uint256 amount)"
        execution_options: "none"
        params:
          - name: amount
            param_type: "static"
            operator: "pass"

      - signature: "function deposit(uint256 amount)"
        execution_options: "none"
        params:
          - name: amount
            param_type: "static"
            operator: "pass"

      - signature: "function queueWithdrawal(uint256 amount)"
        execution_options: "none"
        params:
          - name: amount
            param_type: "static"
            operator: "pass"

      - signature: "function queueFullWithdrawal()"
        execution_options: "none"

      - signature: "function executeWithdrawal(address accountAddress, uint32 expiry)"
        execution_options: "none"
        params:
          - name: accountAddress
            param_type: "static"
            operator: "equal_to_avatar"
          - name: expiry
            param_type: "static"
            operator: "pass"
{%- endfor %}
